{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cff3NyW4vwnzJfVXzbTxy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passes the self-check, which printed `OK`. Its ELPD-LOO on the short smoke fit (100 draws, 1 chain) was −2385.1. The current best model, `heads_default_alternation_ideal`, scores −2390.1, but that comes from a full fit, so the two numbers can't be compared directly yet. The check also said the leave-one-out estimate on this short fit is unreliable (8.8% of trials have high Pareto k), and it couldn't test convergence with one chain. The full admission fit will settle both. Novelty against the other models was not checked.\n\n**Model name:** `bayesian_rigged_coin_personal_switch_belief`\n\n**Hypothesis:** The model starts from a normative account, as the brief asked: people do Bayesian model comparison. A sequence looks random to the extent that a random coin explains it better than a \"rigged\" process. Two rigged processes are considered, equally likely in advance:\n- a coin biased toward one face, with the size of the bias unknown;\n- a coin that tends to repeat or to alternate, with that tendency unknown.\n\nUnder this account, an uneven count of heads and tails and extreme switching in either direction both count as evidence of rigging. The one distortion is in each person's picture of the random coin: they believe it switches sides at their own personal rate, not the fair 50%.\n\n**Why this design:** The strongest problem the critique found is that people pick the more balanced sequence when switch counts are equal far more often than the best model predicts (z = 7.2). In this model that preference falls out of the Bayesian comparison itself, because a biased coin explains a lopsided sequence well. It isn't an extra cue added on. The comparison against a repeat-or-alternate coin is what gives people a preferred switching rate, with very low and very high rates both looking rigged. The model has no length-scaling or periodicity terms, because evidence in the comparison already builds up naturally with sequence length.\n\n**Implementation:** `compute_features` returns two values per pair: the difference in switch counts, and the difference in how likely each sequence is under the rigged processes (averaged over the unknown bias, computed exactly). Each person has their own believed switch rate and their own sensitivity. Both are drawn from population distributions, with 400 slots so the model can predict new participants.\n\nThe three files are in `iter_0/candidate_0/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2946,"cache_read_input_tokens":62624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2946,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"551d8b5a-9669-43f8-ab01-b56f8c88a0b2","uuid":"f24b3085-fd71-4a3b-bdb9-f6425f28c822","timestamp":"2026-10-03T09:09:20.703Z","request_id":"req_011Cff3Ny5XLVG867unirVRu"}
